---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro'
import Term from '../../components/Term.astro'
import { RealGdpLogScale } from '../../components/islands/RealGdpLogScale'
import { GrowthAndShadow } from '../../components/islands/GrowthAndShadow'
import { WhoWorks } from '../../components/islands/WhoWorks'
import { PricesAndRates } from '../../components/islands/PricesAndRates'
import { LaborAndCapital } from '../../components/islands/LaborAndCapital'
import { economy, income } from '../../data'
import { join, routeSections } from '../../data/sections'
import { figuresOf } from '../../data/figures'

/* Figure number, title and source come from the manifest, which /contents reads too. */
const fig = figuresOf('/economy')
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The economy | US federal finance"
  description="Real growth, work, prices and the split between labor and capital, from 1950."
  sections={routeSections['/economy']}
>
  <p class="kicker">Route 1 of 3</p>
  <h1>The economy</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">
    The denominators everything else on this site is measured against, and one thing they do
    not show on their own.
  </p>
  <section id="one-picture">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 1</span></p>
    <h2>Real output, fiscal 1950 to fiscal 2025</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      <Term id="real">Real</Term> <Term id="gross-domestic-product">GDP</Term> was $2.38 trillion
      in fiscal 1950 and $23.72 trillion in fiscal 2025, both
      in <Term id="fiscal-year">FY2025</Term> <Term id="chained-dollars">chained dollars</Term>. That is 9.95
      times as much output, an average of 3.1% a year over 75 years.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Real GDP grew 895% between FY1950 and FY2025, from $2.383T to $23.718T in FY2025 chained
      dollars.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Real GDP grew 895% between fiscal 1950 and fiscal 2025, from $2.38 trillion to $23.72 trillion in FY2025 chained dollars, tracing a near-straight line on a log scale with visible bends at the 2008 to 2009 recession and in fiscal 2020."
      fig={fig('real-gdp')}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="real GDP in $ trillions, FY2025 chained, on a base-10 log scale"
      note="The series continues to FY2036 as a CBO baseline projection. Projected years are drawn as a separate dashed line beyond the marked FY2025 boundary and are not actuals."
    >
      <RealGdpLogScale
        client:visible
        rows={economy.data}
        lastActualFy={economy._meta.estimate_boundary!.last_actual_fy}
      />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      The vertical axis is a base-10 log scale. Equal distances up the axis are equal percentage
      changes rather than equal dollar amounts. A constant growth rate therefore draws as a
      straight line. On a linear axis the last 20 years would tower over the first 50. Every
      earlier deviation would flatten into the baseline. Here the 2008 to 2009 recession and the
      fiscal 2020 dip read as bends in an otherwise near-constant slope.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      Fiscal years, not calendar years. FY2025 ran October 2024 to
      September 2025. The <Term id="congressional-budget-office">CBO</Term> file runs to FY2036.
      Everything after FY2025 is a baseline projection, drawn as a dashed line beyond the marked
      boundary. <Term id="nominal">Nominal</Term> GDP in FY1995 was $7.56 trillion, the same
      denominator the government route divides by.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="growth-shadow">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 2</span></p>
    <h2>Output per hour and median household income</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Between 1984 and 2024, output per hour in the nonfarm business sector rose 116.5%.
      Real <Term id="median">median</Term> household income rose 38.6%. Both are indexed to
      1984 = 100.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Output per hour reached 216.5 by 2024. Real median household income reached 138.6, and the
      two lines separate over the period.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Output per hour reached 216.5 by 2024. Real median household income reached 138.6, and the two lines separate over the period."
      fig={fig('growth-shadow')}
      xUnit="Year (fiscal for output per hour, calendar for median household income)"
      yUnit="index, 1984 = 100"
      note="Two sources on two calendars. Output per hour is CBO, fiscal years; real median household income is Census via FRED, calendar years in 2024 dollars. The two use different price indexes and different units of observation. The vertical gap between the indexed lines is therefore not a measurable quantity."
    >
      <GrowthAndShadow client:visible economyRows={economy.data} incomeRows={income.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      The two lines are not directly commensurable and the gap between them is not a quantity of
      anything. Output per hour is real nonfarm business output divided by hours worked, published
      by CBO on a fiscal year basis. Real median household income is a Census Bureau household
      survey median, published on a calendar year basis in 2024 dollars. They come from different
      sources and are deflated by different price indexes. They also count different units of
      observation, one per hour worked and the other per household.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      The shared window is 1984 to 2024. Median household income does not start until 1984 and
      does not yet run past 2024. The chart is therefore drawn over that window. It does not run
      the full 1950 to 2025 span of the output per hour series. The table below carries the full
      span, with no data marked where the Census series does not reach.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      This chart shows that the two series diverged. It does not show why, and nothing here
      identifies a cause.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="who-works">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 3</span></p>
    <h2>Unemployment, the noncyclical rate and participation</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Unemployment was 4.2% in fiscal 2025 against a noncyclical rate of 4.4%. Labour force
      participation was 62.4%, down from a peak of 67.1% in fiscal 2000.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      In FY2025 unemployment was 4.175% and CBO's noncyclical rate 4.408%, the first below the
      second. Participation was 62.444%, 4.7 points below the FY2000 peak of 67.115%.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Unemployment was 4.2% in fiscal 2025 against a noncyclical rate of 4.4%, while labour force participation was 62.4%, 4.7 points below its fiscal 2000 peak."
      fig={fig('who-works')}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="percent of the labour force (unemployment and the noncyclical rate) and percent of the civilian population 16 and over (participation)"
      note="Fiscal year averages. FY2020 averages 7.3% over October 2019 to September 2020. The monthly peak of spring 2020 is not in this file and is not shown. The noncyclical rate is a CBO estimate, not an observation. The series continues to FY2036 as a CBO baseline projection. Projected years draw as a separate dashed line beyond the marked FY2025 boundary."
    >
      <WhoWorks
        client:visible
        rows={economy.data}
        lastActualFy={economy._meta.estimate_boundary!.last_actual_fy}
      />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      The unemployment rate counts people without work who are looking for it, as a percent of
      the labour force. The noncyclical rate is CBO's estimate of the rate that would prevail at
      the economy's capacity. It is an estimate
      rather than an observation, CBO revises it, and it moves slowly. The two lines share the
      upper panel because they measure the same thing.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      Participation sits in a separate panel because its base is different. It is a percent of the
      civilian population aged 16 and over, and not of the labour force. Putting it on the same
      axis as unemployment would put two denominators on one scale.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      These are fiscal year averages. FY2020 ran October 2019 to September 2020 and averages
      7.3%. The monthly spike of spring 2020 therefore sits inside that average rather than
      appearing as a point. The highest fiscal year in the series is FY1983, at 10.1%. The
      unemployment panel is linear and starts at zero, so nothing is compressed and nothing is
      clipped. The participation panel does not start at zero. It runs over the range the series
      occupies, which its axis states. A zero-based axis would squeeze a move of about eight
      points into a band too small to read.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="prices-rates">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 4</span></p>
    <h2>Inflation and interest rates</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Inflation and the cost of borrowing share a section because neither reads correctly on
      its own. Whether a fed funds rate is high or low depends on what prices were doing at the
      time. The two panels below plot the price series above the rate series over the same fiscal
      years. A reader can then ask that of any year in the run.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      The fed funds rate peaked at 16.9% in fiscal 1981. That was one fiscal year
      after <Term id="consumer-price-index">CPI-U</Term> inflation
      peaked at 13.6% in fiscal 1980.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="The fed funds rate peaked at 16.9% in fiscal 1981. That was one fiscal year after CPI-U inflation peaked at 13.6% in fiscal 1980."
      fig={fig('prices-rates')}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="percent change in the price index from the previous fiscal year (CPI-U and core PCE) and percent per year (the fed funds rate, the 3-month bill and the 10-year note)"
      note="CPI-U and core PCE are published as index levels. The inflation panel plots the year-over-year percent change in each index, not the index itself. CPI-U inflation is derivable from fiscal 1951 and core PCE from fiscal 1961, one year after each index starts. The first index year has no prior year to compare against. The fed funds rate starts fiscal 1955, the 3-month bill fiscal 1950, and the 10-year note fiscal 1954. The series continue to FY2036 as a CBO baseline projection. Projected years draw as a separate dashed branch past the marked FY2025 boundary."
    >
      <PricesAndRates
        client:visible
        rows={economy.data}
        lastActualFy={economy._meta.estimate_boundary!.last_actual_fy}
      />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      CPI-U and <Term id="pce-price-index">core PCE</Term> are published by CBO as index levels,
      not rates. Both panels above
      derive the year-over-year percent change from the index instead. CPI-U inflation is
      derivable from fiscal 1951, one year after the CPI-U index itself starts in fiscal 1950.
      The first index year has no prior year to compare against. Core PCE inflation is derivable
      from fiscal 1961, for the same reason. The core PCE index itself starts fiscal 1960. CPI-U inflation was negative in fiscal 1955 and again in fiscal 2009, when it fell to
      -0.3%. It rose to 7.9% in fiscal 2022 and was 2.7% in fiscal 2025. Core PCE inflation
      peaked at 9.2% in fiscal 1981, was 5.3% in fiscal 2022, and 2.8% in fiscal 2025.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      The rates panel holds three series on one zero-anchored axis, because all three are
      positive throughout. Two are the fed funds rate from fiscal 1955 and the 3-month bill from
      fiscal 1950. The third is the 10-year note from fiscal 1954. The fed funds rate peaked at 16.9% in
      fiscal 1981 and fell to 0.08% in fiscal 2021. It rose again to 4.4% in fiscal 2025. The
      3-month bill fell to 0.03% in fiscal 2015 and was 4.2% in fiscal 2025. The 10-year note
      peaked at 13.9% in fiscal 1982 and fell to 1.1% in fiscal 2020. It was 4.3% in fiscal
      2025. Each
      near-zero value sits on the same axis as the fiscal 1981 peak, not compressed onto a
      separate scale. Each series draws a gap, never a zero, before its own documented start
      year.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      Set against each other, the two panels show the same shape in both. Prices and
      rates fell to near zero between 2009 and 2021 before rising again. Nothing here identifies
      a cause. The panels are stacked for a narrower reason. A rate is only readable next to what
      prices were doing at the time.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      Rates are the mechanism that reprices the debt. The government route shows what that costs,
      in <a href={`${join('/government')}/#net-interest`}>net interest</a>. It also shows how fast
      the repricing feeds through, in <a href={`${join('/government')}/#how-old`}>how old the debt
      is</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="labor-capital">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 5</span></p>
    <h2>The wage and profit shares of GDP</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Wages and salaries and corporate profits are both drawn here as shares of GDP rather than
      of national income. They sum to nothing and are comparable only to themselves over time.
      The two lines settle which way each share has moved since fiscal 1950. They also settle
      whether the two moves are the same size.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Wages and salaries fell from a fiscal 1970 peak of 51.5% of GDP to a fiscal 2024 low of
      42.2%. Corporate profits rose from a fiscal 1982 low of 7.0% of GDP to a fiscal 2025 peak
      of 13.1%.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Wages and salaries fell from a fiscal 1970 peak of 51.5% of GDP to a fiscal 2024 low of 42.2%. Corporate profits rose from a fiscal 1982 low of 7.0% of GDP to a fiscal 2025 peak of 13.1%, both shares of GDP rather than of national income."
      fig={fig('labor-capital')}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="percent of GDP"
      note="Wages and salaries and corporate profits here are shares of GDP rather than of national income. They do not sum to 100 with anything. They are comparable to each other over time, and not to a factor-share decomposition. The fiscal 2020 moves in both series follow a fall in the nominal GDP denominator. Neither follows a change in who was paid what. The series continue to FY2036 as a CBO baseline projection. Projected years draw as a separate dashed branch past the marked FY2025 boundary."
    >
      <LaborAndCapital
        client:visible
        rows={economy.data}
        lastActualFy={economy._meta.estimate_boundary!.last_actual_fy}
      />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      Wages and salaries and corporate profits here are shares of GDP rather than of national
      income. They do not sum to 100 with anything. They are comparable to each other over time,
      and this is not a factor-share decomposition of national income. Wages and salaries
      were 49.1% of GDP in fiscal 1950 and peaked at 51.5% in fiscal 1970. They fell to a low of
      42.2% in fiscal 2024 before ending the series at 42.4% in fiscal 2025. Corporate profits
      were 11.2% of GDP in fiscal 1950 and fell to a low of 7.0% in fiscal 1982. They rose to a
      fiscal 2025 peak of 13.1%.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      The fiscal 2020 moves in both series are pandemic denominator artefacts rather than a
      trend. Wages and salaries were 43.3% of GDP in fiscal 2019 and rose to 44.0% in fiscal
      2020. Nothing about bargaining power moved that fast. Nominal GDP fell in fiscal 2020.
      A share's numerator can rise against a shrinking denominator without any of it being newly
      earned. Corporate profits fell to 11.3% of GDP in fiscal 2020 and rose to 12.7% in fiscal
      2021. That is the same denominator move in the other direction.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="limits">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 6</span></p>
    <h2>Limits of this route</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      This route runs fiscal 1950 to fiscal 2036. It answers what happened to output, prices,
      rates and the wage and profit split across the whole economy. It does not answer who
      received any of it.
    </p>

    <p class="prose">
      <strong>Fiscal-year basis.</strong> Every series on this route is a fiscal year, not a
      calendar year. Fiscal 2025 ran October 2024 to September 2025. A monthly peak or trough
      inside a fiscal year does not appear here as a point. Only the fiscal-year average or
      year-end value does.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      <strong>The projection boundary.</strong> This route's data covers fiscal 1950 to fiscal
      2036. CBO publishes actuals and a baseline projection in one series running to FY2036.
      Everything after fiscal 2025 is that baseline projection rather than an observation. It is
      drawn as a separate dashed branch past a labelled boundary rule on every chart.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      <strong>National aggregates say nothing about distribution.</strong> Every series on this
      route (GDP, prices, rates, the wage and profit shares) is one number for the whole
      economy. None of them says who received it, which households gained or lost, or how any
      of it was distributed.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      <strong>The wage/profit comparison is not a factor-share decomposition.</strong> Section
      5's wages-and-salaries and corporate-profits series are each measured against GDP rather
      than against national income. They do not sum to 100 with anything. They are not a complete
      accounting of who gets paid out of what the economy produces.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      The <a href={`${join('/households')}/`}>households route</a> picks up from here. It covers
      what a household actually earns and how far the spread between households runs. It also
      covers what is paid in tax once <Term id="payroll-tax">payroll tax</Term> is counted.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
